Plato

Intro

Athens, 2,400 years ago. It’s a compact place: only about a quarter of a million
people live here. There are fine baths, theatres, temples, shopping arcades and
gymnasiums. It’s warm for more than half the year. This is also home to the
world’s first true – and probably greatest – philosopher: Plato. Born into a
prominent and wealthy family in the city, Plato devoted his life to one goal:
helping people achieve what he termed εὐδαιμονία (eudaimonia), or fulfilment.
Plato is often confused with Socrates. Socrates was an older friend who taught
Plato a lot but didn’t write any books. Plato wrote lots of them: 36, all
dialogues, beautifully crafted scripts of imaginary discussions in which
Socrates is always given a starring role. Among them are The Republic, The
Symposium, The Laws, The Meno and The Apology. Plato had four big ideas for
making life more fulfilling.

Big Ideas

First big idea: Think more. We rarely give ourselves time to think carefully and
logically about our lives and how to live them. Sometimes we just go along with
what the Greeks called ‘doxa’, or ‘popular opinion’. In the 36 books he wrote,
Plato showed this ‘common sense’ to be riddled with errors, prejudice and
superstition. Fame is great. Follow your heart. Money is the key to a good life.
The problem is that popular opinions steer us towards the wrong values, careers
and relationships. Plato’s answer is ‘Know yourself’. This means engaging in a
special kind of therapy: philosophy. It involves subjecting your ideas to
examination rather than acting on impulse. If you strengthen your
self-knowledge, you’re less likely to be swayed by your feelings. Plato likened
the influence of our feelings to the dangerous pull of a team of wild horses. In
honour of his mentor and friend Socrates, this kind of examination is called a
Socratic discussion. You can have one with yourself or, ideally, with another
person who isn’t trying to catch you out but wants to help you clarify your own
ideas.

Second big idea: Let your lover change you. That sounds weird if you think that
love means finding someone who wants you just the way you are. In The Symposium,
Plato’s dialogue about a dinner party at which a group of friends drink too much
and get talking about love, sex and relationships, Plato says: ‘True love is
admiration.’ In other words, you should choose someone with very good qualities
that you yourself lack. For example, they might be really brave, organised, or
warm and sincere. By getting close to this person, you can become a little more
like them. The right person for us helps us grow to our full potential. For
Plato, in a good relationship, a couple shouldn’t love each other exactly as
they are right now. They should be committed to helping each other grow – and to
enduring the stormy passages this inevitably involves. Each person should want
to seduce the other into becoming a better version of themselves.

The Message of Beauty

Three: Decode the message of beauty. Pretty much everyone likes beautiful
things. But Plato was the first to ask why we like them. He found a fascinating
reason: beautiful objects whisper important truths to us about the good life. We
find things beautiful when we unconsciously sense in them qualities that we need
but lack in our lives: gentleness, harmony, balance, peace and strength.
Beautiful objects therefore have a really important function: they help to
educate our souls. Ugliness is a serious matter, too. It parades dangerous and
damaged characteristics in front of us. It makes it harder to be wise, kind and
calm. Plato sees art as therapeutic: it is the duty of poets and painters (and,
nowadays, novelists, television producers and designers) to help us live good
lives.

Four: Reform society. Plato spent a lot of time thinking about what government
and society should ideally be like. He was the world’s first utopian thinker. In
this, he was inspired by Athens’s great rival, Sparta. This was a city-sized
machine for turning out great soldiers. Everything the Spartans did – how they
raised their children, how their economy was organised, whom they admired, how
they had sex and what they ate – was tailored to that one goal. And Sparta was
hugely successful from a military point of view. But that wasn’t Plato’s
concern. He wanted to know: How could a society become better at producing not
military power but fulfilled people?

In his book The Republic, Plato identifies a number of changes that should be
made. Athenian society was very focused on the rich, such as the louche
aristocrat Alcibiades, and sports celebrities, such as the boxer Milo of Croton.
Plato wasn’t impressed: it really matters whom we admire, because celebrities
influence our outlook, ideas and conduct. Bad role models lend glamour to
character flaws. Plato therefore wanted to give Athens new celebrities,
replacing the current crop with people of wisdom and virtue, whom he called
Guardians. They would serve as models for everyone’s development. These people
would be distinguished by their record of public service, their modesty and
simple habits, their dislike of the limelight, and the breadth and depth of
their experience. They would be the most honoured and admired people in society.
He also wanted to end democracy in Athens. He wasn’t crazy. He simply observed
how few people think properly before they vote, and concluded that this leads to
very substandard rulers. He didn’t want to replace democracy with a horrid
dictatorship; rather, he wanted to prevent people from voting until they had
started to think rationally – until they had become philosophers. Otherwise,
government would simply amount to a kind of mob rule.

To help the process, Plato started a school, the Academy, in Athens, which
lasted for a good 300 years. There, pupils learnt not just maths and spelling
but also how to be good and kind. His ultimate goal was for politicians to
become philosophers: ‘The world will not be right,’ he said, ‘until kings become
philosophers or philosophers kings.’